WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.368
By the end of this lesson, you will be able to
trace one RISC-V ADD instruction from its

2
00:00:06.368 --> 00:00:10.229
thirty-two encoded bits to the value written
into a destination register.

3
00:00:10.229 --> 00:00:16.576
You will also be able to explain why a
pipeline improves throughput, identify data

4
00:00:16.576 --> 00:00:20.656
and control hazards, and distinguish
architectural correctness from implementation

5
00:00:20.656 --> 00:00:21.109
speed.

6
00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:25.867
Our worked instruction is add x5, x6, x7.

7
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:31.947
We will use a classic five-stage teaching
pipeline: Instruction Fetch, Instruction

8
00:00:31.947 --> 00:00:35.816
Decode, Execute, Memory Access, and Write
Back.

9
00:00:35.816 --> 00:00:39.579
That pipeline is not the only possible
processor design.

10
00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:45.170
It is a clear model for seeing how encoded
instructions become coordinated data movement

11
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:45.969
and control.

12
00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:49.155
First, separate the contract from the
machinery.

13
00:00:49.155 --> 00:00:55.958
The RISC-V ISA says what ADD means: read two
source registers, add their values, and write

14
00:00:55.958 --> 00:00:59.359
the low XLEN bits into the destination
register.

15
00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:04.117
It also specifies the instruction fields that
identify that operation.

16
00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:06.158
Software depends on this promise.

17
00:01:06.158 --> 00:01:11.581
Internally, one processor might execute an
instruction in several simple stages, another

18
00:01:11.581 --> 00:01:17.907
might issue multiple instructions at once, and
a small controller might reuse hardware across

19
00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:18.811
many cycles.

20
00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:25.440
Those designs may look very different, yet a
correct implementation must produce the same

21
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:26.387
software-visible result.

22
00:01:26.387 --> 00:01:31.704
This distinction keeps our reasoning
disciplined: begin with what must happen, then

23
00:01:31.704 --> 00:01:34.805
study how one pipeline makes it happen.

24
00:01:34.946 --> 00:01:38.454
The processor receives bits, not assembly
text.

25
00:01:38.454 --> 00:01:45.661
Our instruction word is hexadecimal zero zero
seven three zero two B three.

26
00:01:45.661 --> 00:01:53.445
Reading the R-type fields from bit thirty-one
down to bit zero gives funct seven, rs2, rs1,

27
00:01:53.445 --> 00:01:55.878
funct three, rd, and opcode.

28
00:01:55.878 --> 00:02:00.597
The opcode says this is a register-register
arithmetic instruction.

29
00:02:00.597 --> 00:02:04.819
Funct three and funct seven narrow that family
to ADD.

30
00:02:04.819 --> 00:02:10.444
The rs1 field is binary zero zero one one
zero, which names x6.

31
00:02:10.444 --> 00:02:13.582
The rs2 field names x7.

32
00:02:13.582 --> 00:02:16.209
The rd field names x5.

33
00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:21.468
Decode therefore extracts both the operation
and the register addresses from one

34
00:02:21.468 --> 00:02:22.344
fixed-width word.

35
00:02:22.344 --> 00:02:27.370
The assembly form is a human-readable
rendering of those exact fields.

36
00:02:27.506 --> 00:02:31.767
Assume the program counter contains zero x one
thousand.

37
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:37.336
Fetch presents that address to instruction
memory and receives the thirty-two-bit ADD

38
00:02:37.336 --> 00:02:37.800
word.

39
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:44.042
At the same time, the processor forms the
sequential next address, zero x one thousand

40
00:02:44.042 --> 00:02:44.458
four.

41
00:02:44.458 --> 00:02:51.301
That plus-four step is valid for this RV32I
teaching example because we are not using

42
00:02:51.301 --> 00:02:52.213
compressed instructions.

43
00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:56.907
Fetch does not yet know whether control flow
will remain sequential.

44
00:02:56.907 --> 00:03:00.925
A branch or jump may later redirect the
program counter.

45
00:03:00.925 --> 00:03:06.720
For now, the current address, instruction
bits, and predicted next address must remain

46
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.394
aligned as the instruction moves forward.

47
00:03:09.394 --> 00:03:14.561
Pipeline state records the information the
Decode stage will need on the next clock

48
00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:14.930
cycle.

49
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.783
In Decode, the instruction fields drive two
related jobs.

50
00:03:19.783 --> 00:03:25.293
First, the register file uses rs1 and rs2 as
read addresses.

51
00:03:25.293 --> 00:03:32.313
If x6 contains nineteen and x7 contains
twenty-three, those values appear on the two

52
00:03:32.313 --> 00:03:33.316
read ports.

53
00:03:33.316 --> 00:03:38.329
Second, control logic interprets the opcode
and function fields.

54
00:03:38.329 --> 00:03:44.177
It selects an integer addition, chooses two
register operands for the ALU, disables a

55
00:03:44.177 --> 00:03:48.354
data-memory write, and enables a later
register write to x5.

56
00:03:48.354 --> 00:03:50.765
Data alone is not enough.

57
00:03:50.765 --> 00:03:56.744
The destination register number and every
control decision needed by later stages must

58
00:03:56.744 --> 00:03:58.584
advance beside the operands.

59
00:03:58.584 --> 00:04:03.234
If a value and its control signal become
misaligned, the processor could compute

60
00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:06.454
correctly but write the result to the wrong
place.

61
00:04:06.602 --> 00:04:12.585
The Execute stage supplies nineteen and
twenty-three to the arithmetic logic unit.

62
00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:18.307
Operand-select controls choose the two
register values, and the ALU control selects

63
00:04:18.307 --> 00:04:18.784
addition.

64
00:04:18.784 --> 00:04:20.608
The result is forty-two.

65
00:04:20.608 --> 00:04:28.763
In RV32I, XLEN is thirty-two, so ADD retains
the low thirty-two bits of the mathematical

66
00:04:28.763 --> 00:04:29.307
sum.

67
00:04:29.307 --> 00:04:35.265
If the signed mathematical result exceeds the
signed range, ADD still produces the low-bit

68
00:04:35.265 --> 00:04:38.669
result; it does not raise an
arithmetic-overflow exception.

69
00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:44.728
The ISA defines that behavior, while the ALU
and surrounding control implement it.

70
00:04:44.728 --> 00:04:51.268
For this example there is no overflow, so the
value forty-two and the destination number x5

71
00:04:51.268 --> 00:04:53.312
advance toward the next stage.

72
00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:56.819
ADD does not need a data-memory read or write.

73
00:04:56.819 --> 00:05:02.682
Even so, in this regular five-stage
organization it occupies the Memory stage

74
00:05:02.682 --> 00:05:09.522
while its ALU result, destination register,
and write-enable bit move through the EX slash

75
00:05:09.522 --> 00:05:12.942
MEM and MEM slash WB pipeline registers.

76
00:05:12.942 --> 00:05:17.865
A load instruction would use this stage to
read data, and a store would write data.

77
00:05:17.865 --> 00:05:20.659
ADD simply preserves its result.

78
00:05:20.659 --> 00:05:27.496
In Write Back, a result-select control chooses
the ALU value rather than memory data, the

79
00:05:27.496 --> 00:05:31.143
write-enable signal is asserted, and x5
receives forty-two.

80
00:05:31.143 --> 00:05:36.621
At that moment the instruction's promised
architectural effect becomes visible in the

81
00:05:36.621 --> 00:05:37.534
register file.

82
00:05:37.658 --> 00:05:39.738
Why split the work into stages?

83
00:05:39.738 --> 00:05:44.623
Because different instructions can use
different hardware during the same cycle.

84
00:05:44.623 --> 00:05:50.621
While our ADD is in Execute, a following
independent instruction can be in Decode and

85
00:05:50.621 --> 00:05:52.620
another can be in Fetch.

86
00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.643
During the next cycle, all three advance.

87
00:05:55.643 --> 00:06:01.886
After the pipeline fills, this ideal sequence
can complete roughly one instruction per cycle

88
00:06:01.886 --> 00:06:07.684
even though each instruction spends several
cycles traveling from Fetch to Write Back.

89
00:06:07.684 --> 00:06:13.130
That is a throughput improvement, not proof
that one instruction has lower latency.

90
00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:19.597
The clock period must accommodate the slowest
stage plus pipeline-register overhead, and the

91
00:06:19.597 --> 00:06:24.074
ideal completion rate assumes instructions can
advance without interruption.

92
00:06:24.218 --> 00:06:29.703
Now place sub x8, x5, x9 immediately after the
ADD.

93
00:06:29.703 --> 00:06:36.242
The subtraction needs the new x5 as an input,
but ordinary register-file timing may not

94
00:06:36.242 --> 00:06:39.729
provide that value when the subtraction
reaches Execute.

95
00:06:39.729 --> 00:06:45.632
This is a read-after-write hazard: the later
instruction reads a register that the earlier

96
00:06:45.632 --> 00:06:46.897
instruction will write.

97
00:06:46.897 --> 00:06:52.027
Hazard logic compares the source registers of
younger instructions with destination

98
00:06:52.027 --> 00:06:54.359
registers already in the pipeline.

99
00:06:54.359 --> 00:06:57.522
Detecting the name match is only the first
step.

100
00:06:57.522 --> 00:07:02.662
The processor must also know whether the value
is ready and where it currently resides.

101
00:07:02.662 --> 00:07:08.275
Correctness requires the subtraction to use
forty-two, not the stale value previously

102
00:07:08.275 --> 00:07:09.678
stored in x5.

103
00:07:09.818 --> 00:07:15.826
For an ALU-to-ALU dependency, forwarding can
often avoid a full wait for Write Back.

104
00:07:15.826 --> 00:07:22.713
A bypass path routes the ADD result from a
later pipeline register directly to the

105
00:07:22.713 --> 00:07:24.091
subtraction's ALU input.

106
00:07:24.091 --> 00:07:30.671
The register file may still contain the old
x5, but the operand multiplexer selects the

107
00:07:30.671 --> 00:07:31.987
newer forwarded value.

108
00:07:31.987 --> 00:07:35.074
Forwarding does not solve every dependency.

109
00:07:35.074 --> 00:07:40.941
If a load produces data only after Memory
Access and the next instruction needs it

110
00:07:40.941 --> 00:07:45.635
immediately, the value may arrive too late for
the dependent Execute stage.

111
00:07:45.635 --> 00:07:50.405
Hazard control then freezes selected pipeline
state and inserts a bubble.

112
00:07:50.405 --> 00:07:55.048
One cycle of lost throughput is preferable to
an incorrect result.

113
00:07:55.154 --> 00:07:57.693
Branches create a different uncertainty.

114
00:07:57.693 --> 00:08:03.826
Fetch wants a next address every cycle, but
the branch decision and target may not be

115
00:08:03.826 --> 00:08:04.593
known immediately.

116
00:08:04.593 --> 00:08:06.507
A simple processor can wait.

117
00:08:06.507 --> 00:08:10.614
A faster design may predict a direction and
continue fetching.

118
00:08:10.614 --> 00:08:16.552
If the prediction is wrong, the processor
redirects the program counter and flushes

119
00:08:16.552 --> 00:08:20.206
younger wrong-path instructions before they
change architectural state.

120
00:08:20.206 --> 00:08:23.853
A flush is not merely erasing a diagram box.

121
00:08:23.853 --> 00:08:29.574
It disables the invalid instructions' control
effects so they cannot write registers or

122
00:08:29.574 --> 00:08:30.014
memory.

123
00:08:30.014 --> 00:08:35.626
Prediction can improve average performance,
but recovery logic preserves correctness when

124
00:08:35.626 --> 00:08:37.157
speculation is wrong.

125
00:08:37.298 --> 00:08:40.054
Performance language needs precision.

126
00:08:40.054 --> 00:08:44.975
In an idealized scalar pipeline, the
steady-state cycles per instruction can

127
00:08:44.975 --> 00:08:45.870
approach one.

128
00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:52.017
Real execution includes pipeline filling and
draining, data stalls, control redirects,

129
00:08:52.017 --> 00:08:54.811
cache delays, and other constraints.

130
00:08:54.811 --> 00:09:01.883
A useful first model is actual CPI equals the
ideal baseline plus the average stall cost per

131
00:09:01.883 --> 00:09:02.299
instruction.

132
00:09:02.299 --> 00:09:08.477
This is not a universal performance equation
for every processor; it is a teaching

133
00:09:08.477 --> 00:09:08.918
decomposition.

134
00:09:08.918 --> 00:09:10.844
Clock rate also matters.

135
00:09:10.844 --> 00:09:16.984
Deeper pipelines can shorten some stage
delays, but they add registers, control

136
00:09:16.984 --> 00:09:20.054
complexity, and potentially larger recovery
penalties.

137
00:09:20.054 --> 00:09:26.013
Architecture evaluation therefore considers
both work completed per cycle and the duration

138
00:09:26.013 --> 00:09:27.503
of each cycle.

139
00:09:27.626 --> 00:09:34.068
We began with the word zero x zero zero seven
three zero two B three.

140
00:09:34.068 --> 00:09:42.257
Decode identified ADD, rs1 x6, rs2 x7, and
destination x5.

141
00:09:42.257 --> 00:09:46.032
Fetch read the instruction at zero x one
thousand.

142
00:09:46.032 --> 00:09:49.093
Decode read nineteen and twenty-three.

143
00:09:49.093 --> 00:09:51.262
Execute produced forty-two.

144
00:09:51.262 --> 00:09:55.075
Memory Access performed no data-memory
operation.

145
00:09:55.075 --> 00:09:58.009
Write Back committed forty-two to x5.

146
00:09:58.009 --> 00:10:04.140
Independent instructions could overlap, while
dependencies or branches required forwarding,

147
00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:06.593
stalls, redirects, or flushing.

148
00:10:06.593 --> 00:10:12.285
To test your understanding, ask four questions
about any instruction: What operation do the

149
00:10:12.285 --> 00:10:13.098
bits encode?

150
00:10:13.098 --> 00:10:15.496
Which values enter each stage?

151
00:10:15.496 --> 00:10:18.213
Which control signals must travel with them?

152
00:10:18.213 --> 00:10:23.162
And can any younger instruction proceed
without violating the ISA result?

153
00:10:23.162 --> 00:10:28.831
If you can answer those, you can reason about
the pipeline rather than merely memorize its

154
00:10:28.831 --> 00:10:29.540
five boxes.
